1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,343
Sweetheart, every Christmas dinner in America has

2
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:06,004
a can of jellied cranberry sauce sliced

3
00:00:06,004 --> 00:00:07,232
into little discs,

4
00:00:07,232 --> 00:00:09,551
with the ridges from the can still

5
00:00:09,551 --> 00:00:09,961
on it.

6
00:00:10,141 --> 00:00:11,364
And every year,

7
00:00:11,364 --> 00:00:13,728
half of it goes in the trash.

8
00:00:13,908 --> 00:00:17,100
Homemade cranberry sauce takes ten minutes,

9
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:19,030
costs about three dollars,

10
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:21,256
and it's so good people eat it

11
00:00:21,256 --> 00:00:22,221
with a spoon.

12
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:25,046
But most people make it bitter,

13
00:00:25,046 --> 00:00:26,325
runny, or flat.

14
00:00:26,676 --> 00:00:29,199
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

15
00:00:29,199 --> 00:00:30,972
Christmas cranberry sauce,

16
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:31,858
step by step,

17
00:00:31,858 --> 00:00:34,245
and the five mistakes that ruin it.

18
00:00:34,425 --> 00:00:35,617
And stay with me,

19
00:00:35,617 --> 00:00:38,490
because the last secret is one ingredient

20
00:00:38,490 --> 00:00:40,733
my mother added at the very end,

21
00:00:40,733 --> 00:00:43,256
and it's the reason my Aunt Margaret

22
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:45,709
asked for the recipe every year for

23
00:00:45,709 --> 00:00:47,742
forty years and never got it.

24
00:00:48,092 --> 00:00:50,784
Cranberries are one of the only fruits

25
00:00:50,784 --> 00:00:52,485
that are truly American,

26
00:00:52,485 --> 00:00:52,910
honey.

27
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:55,709
The native people of New England were

28
00:00:55,709 --> 00:00:58,683
eating them long before the Pilgrims came,

29
00:00:58,683 --> 00:01:00,666
and they used them for food,

30
00:01:00,666 --> 00:01:03,144
for medicine, and to dye cloth red.

31
00:01:03,324 --> 00:01:05,954
The sailors called them crane berries,

32
00:01:05,954 --> 00:01:08,929
because the little pink flowers looked like

33
00:01:08,929 --> 00:01:10,313
the head of a crane.

34
00:01:10,493 --> 00:01:13,314
Over time, it became cranberry.

35
00:01:13,665 --> 00:01:16,174
The canned sauce came along in nineteen

36
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:19,262
twelve, from a cranberry grower in Massachusetts

37
00:01:19,262 --> 00:01:21,579
who didn't want to waste his bruised

38
00:01:21,579 --> 00:01:22,093
berries.

39
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:24,107
By the nineteen forties,

40
00:01:24,107 --> 00:01:26,627
it was on every table in America.

41
00:01:26,807 --> 00:01:28,514
My mother wouldn't touch it.

42
00:01:28,694 --> 00:01:29,834
She said, Ruthie,

43
00:01:29,834 --> 00:01:31,981
anything that comes out of a can

44
00:01:31,981 --> 00:01:33,792
in the shape of a can isn't

45
00:01:33,792 --> 00:01:34,127
food.

46
00:01:34,307 --> 00:01:35,828
It's a doorstop.

47
00:01:36,179 --> 00:01:38,361
In our house on Elm Street,

48
00:01:38,361 --> 00:01:41,432
the cranberry sauce was always made on

49
00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:43,694
Christmas Eve, after supper,

50
00:01:43,694 --> 00:01:46,523
in my grandmother's old enamel pot.

51
00:01:46,703 --> 00:01:48,936
Danny and I got to listen for

52
00:01:48,936 --> 00:01:49,629
the pops.

53
00:01:49,809 --> 00:01:52,990
Because when you cook cranberries,

54
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:54,487
honey, they pop,

55
00:01:54,487 --> 00:01:55,517
one by one,

56
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:57,669
like tiny firecrackers.

57
00:01:57,849 --> 00:01:59,900
In a minute I'll tell you why

58
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,668
that pop matters so much.

59
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:04,391
And cranberries were what got a lot

60
00:02:04,391 --> 00:02:06,899
of families through the winter in the

61
00:02:06,899 --> 00:02:08,323
old days, sweetheart.

62
00:02:08,503 --> 00:02:10,649
They keep for months in a cold

63
00:02:10,649 --> 00:02:13,367
cellar, and they're full of vitamin C.

64
00:02:13,547 --> 00:02:16,021
The whaling ships out of New England

65
00:02:16,021 --> 00:02:18,427
carried barrels of them to keep the

66
00:02:18,427 --> 00:02:20,351
sailors from getting scurvy.

67
00:02:20,531 --> 00:02:23,138
Grandma Pearl kept a crock of them

68
00:02:23,138 --> 00:02:25,054
in her cellar all winter,

69
00:02:25,054 --> 00:02:26,511
under a damp cloth,

70
00:02:26,511 --> 00:02:28,275
and they never spoiled.

71
00:02:28,625 --> 00:02:30,401
Here's what you need.

72
00:02:30,581 --> 00:02:34,099
One twelve-ounce bag of fresh cranberries.

73
00:02:34,279 --> 00:02:35,858
One cup of sugar.

74
00:02:36,038 --> 00:02:38,093
Half a cup of water and half

75
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:40,148
a cup of fresh orange juice.

76
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:42,220
The zest of one orange.

77
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:43,863
A pinch of salt.

78
00:02:44,043 --> 00:02:46,346
And a cinnamon stick if you like

79
00:02:46,346 --> 00:02:46,562
it.

80
00:02:46,742 --> 00:02:47,764
That's it.

81
00:02:47,944 --> 00:02:50,224
You'll have about two and a half

82
00:02:50,224 --> 00:02:52,220
cups, enough for ten people,

83
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,144
and it keeps for two weeks.

84
00:02:54,494 --> 00:02:57,024
And here's mistake number one.

85
00:02:57,204 --> 00:02:59,271
Not picking through the berries.

86
00:02:59,451 --> 00:03:01,729
Every bag has a few soft,

87
00:03:01,729 --> 00:03:04,008
brown, or shriveled ones,

88
00:03:04,008 --> 00:03:06,104
and a few little stems.

89
00:03:06,284 --> 00:03:09,054
One bad berry makes the whole pot

90
00:03:09,054 --> 00:03:10,986
taste bitter and musty.

91
00:03:11,166 --> 00:03:13,388
Pour them into a bowl of cold

92
00:03:13,388 --> 00:03:13,848
water.

93
00:03:14,028 --> 00:03:15,679
The good ones float,

94
00:03:15,679 --> 00:03:17,000
and they bounce.

95
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:19,734
Throw out anything soft or dark.

96
00:03:19,914 --> 00:03:21,562
It takes two minutes.

97
00:03:21,913 --> 00:03:24,937
Frozen cranberries work just as well,

98
00:03:24,937 --> 00:03:25,837
sweetheart.

99
00:03:26,017 --> 00:03:28,400
Buy four or five bags in November

100
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:29,772
when they're cheap,

101
00:03:29,772 --> 00:03:31,938
and throw them in the freezer.

102
00:03:32,118 --> 00:03:33,557
Don't thaw them.

103
00:03:33,737 --> 00:03:35,908
Just put them straight in the pot.

104
00:03:36,088 --> 00:03:39,320
My mother bought ten bags every Thanksgiving

105
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:41,963
and made cranberry bread all winter.

106
00:03:42,314 --> 00:03:45,464
And use a stainless steel or enamel

107
00:03:45,464 --> 00:03:48,885
pot, honey, not aluminum or cast iron.

108
00:03:49,065 --> 00:03:51,114
Cranberries are very sour,

109
00:03:51,114 --> 00:03:53,953
and the acid reacts with bare metal.

110
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:56,415
It turns the sauce a dull brown

111
00:03:56,415 --> 00:03:58,844
color and gives it a tinny taste,

112
00:03:58,844 --> 00:04:00,390
like licking a spoon.

113
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:03,614
Grandma Pearl's enamel pot was white with

114
00:04:03,614 --> 00:04:04,356
a red rim,

115
00:04:04,356 --> 00:04:06,880
and it's sitting on my stove right

116
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:07,176
now.

117
00:04:07,356 --> 00:04:10,073
I've never made it in anything else.

118
00:04:10,424 --> 00:04:11,543
Put the sugar,

119
00:04:11,543 --> 00:04:13,783
the water, the orange juice,

120
00:04:13,783 --> 00:04:15,862
and the pinch of salt in a

121
00:04:15,862 --> 00:04:16,182
pot.

122
00:04:16,362 --> 00:04:18,905
Stir it over medium heat until the

123
00:04:18,905 --> 00:04:21,073
sugar melts and it comes to a

124
00:04:21,073 --> 00:04:21,447
boil.

125
00:04:21,627 --> 00:04:24,504
Then add the cranberries and the cinnamon

126
00:04:24,504 --> 00:04:24,924
stick.

127
00:04:25,104 --> 00:04:28,095
Mistake number two is putting the berries

128
00:04:28,095 --> 00:04:30,793
and the sugar in cold water together.

129
00:04:30,973 --> 00:04:32,590
Melt the sugar first,

130
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:35,054
so the berries cook in syrup and

131
00:04:35,054 --> 00:04:36,825
stay bright and glossy.

132
00:04:37,175 --> 00:04:38,463
Now listen.

133
00:04:38,643 --> 00:04:40,278
In about four minutes,

134
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:41,615
they start to pop.

135
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:43,688
Pop, pop, pop.

136
00:04:43,868 --> 00:04:46,697
That's the skins bursting and letting out

137
00:04:46,697 --> 00:04:49,113
the pectin, which is what makes the

138
00:04:49,113 --> 00:04:50,079
sauce thicken.

139
00:04:50,259 --> 00:04:52,419
Let them cook at a gentle boil

140
00:04:52,419 --> 00:04:54,002
for about ten minutes,

141
00:04:54,002 --> 00:04:55,658
until most have popped.

142
00:04:55,838 --> 00:04:58,253
And here's mistake number three.

143
00:04:58,433 --> 00:04:59,988
Cooking it too long.

144
00:05:00,168 --> 00:05:03,566
Twenty minutes, and it turns into thick,

145
00:05:03,566 --> 00:05:05,010
dark, bitter jam.

146
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:07,248
If this is helping you,

147
00:05:07,248 --> 00:05:09,465
tap the like button for me,

148
00:05:09,465 --> 00:05:09,957
honey.

149
00:05:10,137 --> 00:05:12,889
It helps Grandma more than you know.

150
00:05:13,069 --> 00:05:15,089
And mistake number four.

151
00:05:15,269 --> 00:05:17,528
Taking it off the heat because it

152
00:05:17,528 --> 00:05:18,624
looks too runny.

153
00:05:18,804 --> 00:05:21,455
It's supposed to look runny when it's

154
00:05:21,455 --> 00:05:21,741
hot.

155
00:05:21,921 --> 00:05:22,921
As it cools,

156
00:05:22,921 --> 00:05:24,503
the pectin sets it,

157
00:05:24,503 --> 00:05:26,751
and it gets twice as thick.

158
00:05:26,931 --> 00:05:28,850
If it's thick in the pot,

159
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:31,076
it'll be cement on the table.

160
00:05:31,426 --> 00:05:34,084
Here's how to know when it's ready,

161
00:05:34,084 --> 00:05:34,920
sweetheart.

162
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,634
Dip a cold metal spoon into the

163
00:05:37,634 --> 00:05:39,268
pot and lift it out.

164
00:05:39,448 --> 00:05:42,096
If the sauce coats the back of

165
00:05:42,096 --> 00:05:44,920
the spoon and slides off slowly,

166
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:45,891
in a sheet,

167
00:05:45,891 --> 00:05:46,774
it's done.

168
00:05:46,954 --> 00:05:48,848
If it runs off like water,

169
00:05:48,848 --> 00:05:50,669
give it two more minutes.

170
00:05:50,849 --> 00:05:52,614
Then turn off the heat.

171
00:05:52,794 --> 00:05:55,245
It's the same test my mother used

172
00:05:55,245 --> 00:05:57,473
for jelly, and it never fails.

173
00:05:57,823 --> 00:06:00,028
Now the secret I promised you.

174
00:06:00,208 --> 00:06:01,741
Take it off the heat,

175
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:03,784
pull out the cinnamon stick,

176
00:06:03,784 --> 00:06:05,828
and stir in the orange zest.

177
00:06:06,008 --> 00:06:08,248
And then my mother's secret.

178
00:06:08,428 --> 00:06:11,346
One tablespoon of butter and a splash

179
00:06:11,346 --> 00:06:12,213
of vanilla.

180
00:06:12,393 --> 00:06:15,104
The butter makes it glossy and smooth

181
00:06:15,104 --> 00:06:17,375
and takes away that sharp edge,

182
00:06:17,375 --> 00:06:19,939
and the vanilla makes it taste rich

183
00:06:19,939 --> 00:06:21,844
and round, like a dessert.

184
00:06:22,194 --> 00:06:25,655
Aunt Margaret asked my mother every Christmas,

185
00:06:25,655 --> 00:06:27,987
Dorothy, what do you put in it?

186
00:06:28,167 --> 00:06:30,516
And my mother always said,

187
00:06:30,516 --> 00:06:31,871
love, Margaret.

188
00:06:32,051 --> 00:06:33,606
Just love.

189
00:06:33,786 --> 00:06:35,973
I didn't find out myself until I

190
00:06:35,973 --> 00:06:38,432
was thirty, when I watched my mother

191
00:06:38,432 --> 00:06:41,165
sneak that butter in while Aunt Margaret

192
00:06:41,165 --> 00:06:42,668
was in the other room.

193
00:06:42,848 --> 00:06:45,228
Aunt Margaret died not knowing.

194
00:06:45,408 --> 00:06:47,001
And now you know,

195
00:06:47,001 --> 00:06:48,032
sweetheart.

196
00:06:48,382 --> 00:06:50,210
And for a pretty table,

197
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:52,596
here's my mother's mold trick.

198
00:06:52,776 --> 00:06:55,246
Lightly oil a small bundt pan or

199
00:06:55,246 --> 00:06:56,790
a pretty glass bowl,

200
00:06:56,790 --> 00:06:58,489
pour in the hot sauce,

201
00:06:58,489 --> 00:07:00,264
and chill it overnight.

202
00:07:00,444 --> 00:07:01,673
On Christmas Day,

203
00:07:01,673 --> 00:07:03,986
dip the bottom in warm water for

204
00:07:03,986 --> 00:07:06,298
ten seconds and turn it out onto

205
00:07:06,298 --> 00:07:06,876
a plate.

206
00:07:07,056 --> 00:07:09,460
It comes out in a shiny ruby

207
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:09,889
ring.

208
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:12,185
Put a little sprig of rosemary in

209
00:07:12,185 --> 00:07:12,890
the middle.

210
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:14,870
People will gasp.

211
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:16,903
Mistake number five.

212
00:07:17,083 --> 00:07:18,209
Serving it hot.

213
00:07:18,389 --> 00:07:20,979
Cranberry sauce is best cold,

214
00:07:20,979 --> 00:07:23,033
or at room temperature.

215
00:07:23,213 --> 00:07:25,708
Make it at least one day ahead,

216
00:07:25,708 --> 00:07:27,559
put it in a glass bowl,

217
00:07:27,559 --> 00:07:29,813
cover it, and keep it in the

218
00:07:29,813 --> 00:07:30,377
icebox.

219
00:07:30,557 --> 00:07:33,470
It thickens and the flavor gets deeper

220
00:07:33,470 --> 00:07:34,237
overnight.

221
00:07:34,417 --> 00:07:35,708
On Christmas Day,

222
00:07:35,708 --> 00:07:37,684
all you have to do is take

223
00:07:37,684 --> 00:07:38,747
the cover off.

224
00:07:39,098 --> 00:07:41,187
And use the leftovers,

225
00:07:41,187 --> 00:07:41,756
honey.

226
00:07:41,936 --> 00:07:44,314
Spread it on a turkey sandwich with

227
00:07:44,314 --> 00:07:46,081
cream cheese the next day.

228
00:07:46,261 --> 00:07:49,129
Stir it into oatmeal or plain yogurt.

229
00:07:49,309 --> 00:07:51,413
Spoon it over a block of cream

230
00:07:51,413 --> 00:07:54,078
cheese with crackers for an easy party

231
00:07:54,078 --> 00:07:54,498
snack.

232
00:07:54,678 --> 00:07:56,874
Or mix it with a little mustard

233
00:07:56,874 --> 00:07:58,219
for a glaze on ham.

234
00:07:58,399 --> 00:08:01,061
My husband put it on his pancakes

235
00:08:01,061 --> 00:08:03,078
on December twenty-sixth,

236
00:08:03,078 --> 00:08:04,529
every single year.

237
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:06,310
Once you know how,

238
00:08:06,310 --> 00:08:08,455
change it any way you like.

239
00:08:08,635 --> 00:08:11,140
Add a handful of chopped pecans or

240
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,349
walnuts at the end for crunch.

241
00:08:13,529 --> 00:08:15,873
Add a chopped apple or pear with

242
00:08:15,873 --> 00:08:17,705
the berries for a softer,

243
00:08:17,705 --> 00:08:18,730
sweeter sauce.

244
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:21,221
A splash of port wine or bourbon

245
00:08:21,221 --> 00:08:22,521
for the grown-ups.

246
00:08:22,701 --> 00:08:24,973
Or a little grated fresh ginger,

247
00:08:24,973 --> 00:08:27,032
which my granddaughter loves.

248
00:08:27,212 --> 00:08:28,593
It's hard to ruin,

249
00:08:28,593 --> 00:08:31,356
sweetheart, once you know the rules.

250
00:08:31,707 --> 00:08:34,075
And here's my favorite way to give

251
00:08:34,075 --> 00:08:34,981
it as a gift.

252
00:08:35,161 --> 00:08:36,612
Make a double batch,

253
00:08:36,612 --> 00:08:39,224
pour it into little jelly jars while

254
00:08:39,224 --> 00:08:41,111
it's hot, put on the lids,

255
00:08:41,111 --> 00:08:43,433
and tie a red ribbon around each

256
00:08:43,433 --> 00:08:45,029
one with a little tag.

257
00:08:45,209 --> 00:08:48,112
It keeps two weeks in the icebox.

258
00:08:48,292 --> 00:08:50,501
I give one to every neighbor on

259
00:08:50,501 --> 00:08:52,923
our street, and they think I'm the

260
00:08:52,923 --> 00:08:54,491
fanciest cook in Ohio.

261
00:08:54,671 --> 00:08:57,075
It costs about a dollar a jar.

262
00:08:57,425 --> 00:09:00,271
One more idea for Christmas morning,

263
00:09:00,271 --> 00:09:00,745
honey.

264
00:09:00,925 --> 00:09:03,352
Stir a big spoonful of the sauce

265
00:09:03,352 --> 00:09:06,006
into your pancake or muffin batter,

266
00:09:06,006 --> 00:09:08,281
or swirl it into a coffee cake

267
00:09:08,281 --> 00:09:09,342
before baking.

268
00:09:09,522 --> 00:09:11,997
Little red streaks all through it,

269
00:09:11,997 --> 00:09:14,108
and it tastes like Christmas.

270
00:09:14,288 --> 00:09:17,519
I make cranberry orange muffins every Christmas

271
00:09:17,519 --> 00:09:19,375
morning with the leftovers,

272
00:09:19,375 --> 00:09:22,124
and my grandchildren think I bought them

273
00:09:22,124 --> 00:09:22,949
at a bakery.

274
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,704
So one more time.

275
00:09:24,884 --> 00:09:27,601
Pick over your berries in cold water.

276
00:09:27,781 --> 00:09:29,662
Melt the sugar first.

277
00:09:29,842 --> 00:09:32,564
Cook it just until the berries pop,

278
00:09:32,564 --> 00:09:33,963
about ten minutes.

279
00:09:34,143 --> 00:09:36,651
Don't worry if it looks runny.

280
00:09:36,831 --> 00:09:38,739
Stir in the orange zest,

281
00:09:38,739 --> 00:09:40,964
the butter, and the vanilla.

282
00:09:41,144 --> 00:09:42,990
And make it a day ahead.

283
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:46,208
That's cranberry sauce your family will eat

284
